            <ArticleTitle>Contribution of ultrasound in the assessment of patients with suspect idiopathic pudendal nerve disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1278-84</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinph.2013.10.053</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1388-2457(13)01229-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess if Ultrasound (US) is contributive in patients suspected of having idiopathic pudendal neuralgia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between July 2012 and April 2013, 10 consecutive female patients with suspected idiopathic pudendal neuralgia (mean age: 47±14 years; mean BMI: 24±3) were included. Two radiologists blinded to the clinical and neurophysiological data performed pudendal nerve evaluation with broadband linear array transducers (12-7 MHZ, and 17-5 MHZ). MRI was added to confirm US data. A third independent clinician, who did not perform electrodiagnosis and US, reviewed the data and scored US as &quot;contributive&quot; or &quot;non-contributive&quot;: if US confirmed the clinical and neurophysiological diagnosis or if US findings were not useful.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Ultrasound identified alterations to the pudendal nerve in 7/10 of cases (70%). In seven cases US revealed the presence of a diffusely or focally enlarged pudendal nerve confirmed by MRI. In these cases neurophysiological findings were suspicious for pudendal neuralgia in 5/7 cases, whereas in 2/7 cases they were inconclusive.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">High-resolution ultrasound (US) may demonstrate alterations to the pudendal nerve in patients with pudendal neuralgia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SIGNIFICANCE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">US is useful in patients with suspected idiopathic pudendal nerve disease.</AbstractText>
